# Autoload access inside addons (ADR 0017)

## The rule

Scripts under `synesyn_client/addons/**` must **never** name a project autoload
by bare global identifier.

```gdscript
# WRONG — fails to compile wherever the autoload is not live
if ContentBus and ContentBus.has_method("refresh_active_sources"):
    ContentBus.refresh_active_sources()
```

```gdscript
# RIGHT — resolve through the scene tree at use time
const SynTreeAutoloads = preload("res://addons/syntree/scripts/autoload/syntree_autoloads.gd")

var bus := SynTreeAutoloads.content_bus()
if is_instance_valid(bus) and bus.has_method("refresh_active_sources"):
    bus.refresh_active_sources()
```

## Why this is not obvious

A bare autoload identifier only resolves while that autoload is **live**, not
merely declared in `project.godot`. In the editor autoloads are always live, so
the wrong form compiles, autocompletes, and shows method signatures — there is no
local feedback at all. It breaks only later, somewhere else: in a fresh drop-in
project, or in headless tooling that loads scripts before autoloads register.

Assume you will get no warning from the editor. Follow the rule by construction.

## Which resolver

| Autoload owner | Resolver |
|---|---|
| SynTree (`ContentBus`, `PluginManager`) | `res://addons/syntree/scripts/autoload/syntree_autoloads.gd` |
| Host / project (`ThemeManager`, `WorkspaceBootstrapper`, `Global`, `StoryState`, `VoiceService`) | `res://addons/synesyn_core/scripts/synesyn_autoload_access.gd` |

Add a new accessor to the appropriate resolver rather than reaching for the bare
name. Those two files are the only ones exempt from the rule.

Note that a script declaring `class_name X` **cannot** also be autoloaded as `X`:
Godot rejects it with "must not collide with an existing global script class
name". `NotificationManager` is exactly this case — it is a global class,
instantiated directly, and must not be added to any `REQUIRED_AUTOLOADS`.

## Two things that will bite you

**Never cache a resolved autoload in a field.** Resolve at every use. An autoload
that registers later would leave a cached value null forever.

```gdscript
# WRONG
var _bus = SynTreeAutoloads.content_bus()   # captured once, possibly null forever

# RIGHT
func _refresh() -> void:
    var bus := SynTreeAutoloads.content_bus()
```

**Do not use `:=` on values that come back through a resolver.** Accessors return
`Node`, so method calls on them yield `Variant` and inference fails with
"Cannot infer the type". Annotate explicitly (see also the project's typing rules).

```gdscript
# WRONG
var gfm := _global().get_game_flow_manager() if _global() else null
# RIGHT
var gfm: Object = _global().get_game_flow_manager() if _global() else null
```

Where a concrete type matters, give the resolver a `preload`ed script constant as
its return type — that keeps call sites statically checked and exposes the
autoload's enums without a live instance:

```gdscript
const ContentBusScript = preload("res://addons/syntree/scripts/autoload/content_bus.gd")

static func content_bus() -> ContentBusScript:
    return _node("ContentBus") as ContentBusScript

# enums remain reachable with no instance:
await bus.request_view_change(ContentBusScript.ViewMode.LIST)
```

## Also: addons provision their own autoloads

An addon that requires an autoload registers it in its own `EditorPlugin`
`_enter_tree()`, and only removes in `_exit_tree()` what it actually added, so
disabling the plugin never strips a host project's own entries. See
`addons/syntree/syntree_plugin.gd` (`REQUIRED_AUTOLOADS`) for the pattern. Do not
expect the host to hand-edit `project.godot`.

## Verify before reporting done

```bash
# fast, no Godot needed — reads autoload names from project.godot
python utilities/checks/check_addon_autoloads.py

# ground truth: compiles every addon script with no autoloads live
cp utilities/checks/addon_compile_sweep.gd synesyn_client/
<godot> --headless --quit-after 3 --path synesyn_client \
        --script res://addon_compile_sweep.gd
```

Resolve the Godot binary from `godotTools.editorPath.godot4` in
`%APPDATA%\Cursor\User\settings.json`.
